  <title>Installing ChatGPT Desktop on Mint 20 / Ubuntu 20.04 — when the blocker isn&#39;t glibc</title>
  <link>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-09-22-chatgpt-desktop-on-mint-20/</link>
  <pubDate>Tue, 22 Sep 2026 16:16:26 &#43;0800</pubDate>
  <author>william</author>
  <guid>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-09-22-chatgpt-desktop-on-mint-20/</guid>
  <description><![CDATA[<p>OpenAI shipped an official ChatGPT Desktop for Linux in August 2026 (preview),
supporting Ubuntu 24.04/26.04, Debian 13, Fedora 43/44 and Arch. Mint 20.3
(focal, glibc 2.31) is nowhere on that list, so I expected a rerun of the
<a href="/2026-09-22-claude-desktop-on-ubuntu-20.04/">Claude Desktop surgery</a>.</p>
<p>It wasn&rsquo;t. The glibc floor was fine all along — the package is blocked by three
<strong>library renames</strong> and one optional TPM module. The fix is smaller, and the
audit is worth showing because the conclusion is the opposite of last time.</p>]]></description>
</item>
<item>
  <title>Installing Claude Desktop on Ubuntu 20.04 / Mint 20 (glibc 2.31)</title>
  <link>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-09-22-claude-desktop-on-ubuntu-20.04/</link>
  <pubDate>Tue, 22 Sep 2026 14:45:00 &#43;0800</pubDate>
  <author>william</author>
  <guid>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-09-22-claude-desktop-on-ubuntu-20.04/</guid>
  <description><![CDATA[<p>Anthropic&rsquo;s official Claude Desktop <code>.deb</code> declares <code>libc6 (&gt;= 2.34)</code>, which
means Ubuntu 22.04+ / Mint 21+. On Mint 20.3 (focal, glibc 2.31) <code>apt</code> refuses
to install it. It turns out that dependency is an overstatement, and the app
runs fine on glibc 2.31 once you know which three files actually need 2.34.</p>]]></description>
</item>
